    {
      "filename": "Inverness, Capital of the Highlands (40440582462).jpg",
      "title": "File:Inverness, Capital of the Highlands (40440582462).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b2/Inverness%2C_Capital_of_the_Highlands_%2840440582462%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inverness,_Capital_of_the_Highlands_(40440582462).jpg",
      "width": 4753,
      "height": 3449,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Mike McBey",
      "credit": "Inverness, Capital of the Highlands",
      "source": "Inverness, Capital of the Highlands (40440582462)",
      "description": "This is Inverness in the North of Scotland (looking North). The city takes it's name from the River Ness which flows from the famous Loch of that name, through the centre of the city and into the Moray Firth, which is crossed by the Kessock Bridge.  \nIt's the Northernmost city in the United Kingdon and has a population of approx  47,000.  In 2014 and 2015 a survey described Inverness as the happiest place in Scotland and the second happiest in the UK. \n\nInverness is the home of Raigmore Hospital - which is the main hospital for the entire North-West of Scotland - an area of 9,906 sq miles (25,657 km2 . The nearest other big hospitals are in Aberdeen, 100 miles away by road.",
      "dateCreated": "2009-06-13 14:39",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "inverness-wk",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gfhz",
      "mediumPath": "_m/g/f/h/z/inverness-wk/inverness-north-scotla-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/f/h/z/inverness-wk/inverness-north-scotla-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Inverness airport (41461531092).jpg",
      "title": "File:Inverness airport (41461531092).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Inverness_airport_%2841461531092%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inverness_airport_(41461531092).jpg",
      "width": 3928,
      "height": 1744,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Mike McBey",
      "credit": "Inverness airport",
      "source": "Inverness airport (41461531092)",
      "description": "This is the main airport in the North of Scotland (after Aberdeen which is on the North-east coast.)\nAlthough of modest size it plays a vital link with the islands of Orkney, Shetland and the Western Isles. It also links the north of Scotland with the Scottish Central belt, England and beyond into Europe.\nThe first airfield built here was for the RAF in 1940. \nIn 1947 it became available  for civilian use and has slowly grown since then. \nIn the centre-left of the picture is the main terminal building which is not large but arguably offers a more uncomplex and personal service than the gigantic terminals of large airports.\n\nIn my photo, taken as we came into land, we can also see a variety of executive aircraft and private craft as well as an Airbus of the low-cost airline easyJet about to board for a flight to London.",
      "dateCreated": "2009-06-13 14:42",
      "sourceType": "core",
      "sourceArticleId": "inverness-wk",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gfhz",
      "mediumPath": "_m/g/f/h/z/inverness-wk/main-airport-north-sco-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/f/h/z/inverness-wk/main-airport-north-sco-small.webp"
    },
